{
  "title": "Switchboard Signal Variety",
  "description": "## Task\n\nA switchboard has several levers. Pulling a lever mixes its signal level into the output by bitwise XOR; each lever may be pulled or left alone, and the output starts at `0`. Count how many different output values can be produced across all choices of levers (pulling none is a choice).\n\n## Input\n\n- `levels` — the signal level of each lever.\n\n## Output\n\nThe number of distinct XOR values over all subsets of `levels`, empty subset included.\n\n## Example\n\n```python\nswitchboard_signal_variety([1, 2, 3])  # returns 4\nswitchboard_signal_variety([5, 5])  # returns 2\nswitchboard_signal_variety([])  # returns 1\n```\n\n## Constraints\n\n- 0 ≤ length of `levels` ≤ 2000\n- 0 ≤ each level < 2^30\n- Trying every subset is far too slow for lists of this length; the answer is always a power of two.\n\n",
  "signature": "def switchboard_signal_variety(levels: list[int]) -> int",
  "topic": "xor linear basis",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "category": "math-bits",
